'''Anthony Steven Evers''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|iː|v|ər|z}} {{Respell|EE|vərz}};<ref name="WISCPronunciation">{{cite news |last=Tomsyck |first=Teymour |title=NRA campaign ad mispronounces name of Walker opponent Evers |url=https://www.channel3000.com/news/nra-campaign-ad-mispronounces-name-of-walker-opponent-evers-1/806386350 |work=[[WISC-TV]] |date=October 12, 2018 |access-date=October 20, 2018 |archive-date=October 21, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181021060152/https://www.channel3000.com/news/nra-campaign-ad-mispronounces-name-of-walker-opponent-evers-1/806386350 |url-status=dead}}</ref> born November 5, 1951) is an American politician and educator serving since 2019 as the 46th [[governor of Wisconsin]].<ref name="GovBio">{{cite web |title=About the administration |url=https://evers.wi.gov/Pages/About/About_Tony.aspx |publisher=Office of the Governor of Wisconsin |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he served from 2009 to 2019 as Wisconsin's 26th [[Wisconsin Superintendent of Public Instruction|superintendent of public instruction]].<ref name="UWRegentsBio">{{cite web |title=Tony Evers |url=https://www.wisconsin.edu/regents/about-the-regents/tony-evers/ |publisher=[[Universities of Wisconsin|University of Wisconsin System]] Board of Regents |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Born and raised in [[Plymouth, Wisconsin]], Evers was educated at the [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]], where he earned bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degrees in educational administration.<ref name="UWRegentsBio"/> After working as a teacher, he became a school administrator, serving as a principal and later as a district superintendent.<ref name="UWRegentsBio"/> Evers ran unsuccessfully for Superintendent of Public Instruction in 1993, 1997, and 2001.<ref name="PolitiFactCareer">{{cite news |last=Beck |first=Molly |title=Wisconsin Gov. Evers is not a career politician |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2022/oct/07/tim-michels/wisconsin-gov-evers-not-career-politician/ |work=[[PolitiFact]] |date=October 7, 2022 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="TMJ4Career">{{cite news |title=Tony Evers wins reelection as Wisconsin governor, Michels concedes |url=https://www.tmj4.com/decision2022/tim-michels-concedes-wisconsin-governor-race-leaving-evers-as-winner |work=[[WTMJ-TV]] |date=November 8, 2022 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="BlueBook1997">{{cite report |title=Wisconsin Blue Book 1997–1998: Elections |url=http://www.legis.wisconsin.gov/lrb/bb/97bb/ch10.pdf |publisher=Wisconsin Legislative Reference Bureau |date=1998 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |archive-date=May 28, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100528041327/http://www.legis.wisconsin.gov/lrb/bb/97bb/ch10.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> After his 2001 loss, state superintendent [[Elizabeth Burmaster]] appointed him deputy superintendent, a position he held from 2001 to 2009.<ref name="UWRegentsBio"/> In 2009, he ran for Superintendent of Public Instruction again and won. He was reelected in 2013 and 2017.<ref name="GovBio"/>

In August 2017, Evers announced that he would run for governor of Wisconsin in the [[2018 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2018 election]]. He won the crowded Democratic primary in August 2018 with 41.8% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news |last=DeFour |first=Matthew |title=It's Evers: State schools superintendent to challenge Scott Walker in November |url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/it-s-evers-state-schools-superintendent-to-challenge-scott-walker/article_eeaa00ad-5e99-52bd-9085-dedbd0e8da8e.html |newspaper=[[Wisconsin State Journal]] |date=August 14, 2018 |access-date=August 15, 2018}}</ref> Incumbent Republican governor [[Scott Walker (politician)|Scott Walker]] was seen as vulnerable and had been criticized for his education policies.<ref name="NewYorker2018"/> Evers defeated Walker by a margin of 1.1% and was reelected by a larger margin of 3.4% in [[2022 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2022]], defeating Republican nominee Tim Michels.<ref name="AP2018Win">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Democrat Tony Evers defeats Wisconsin Gov. Scott Walker |url=https://apnews.com/gubernatorial-elections-8ba8b7cd0cf14082a11741b74ae752d6 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=November 6, 2018 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="MJS2022Win">{{cite news |last=Beck |first=Molly |title=Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers defeats Tim Michels to win second term in 2022 midterm election |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/11/09/tony-evers-defeats-tim-michels-in-2022-wisconsin-governor-election/69597058007/ |access-date=November 9, 2022 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref><ref name="AP2022Win">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin Gov. Evers defeats foe he called democracy threat |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-race-2022-midterm-elections-f3639e6ef8c9058511cef47a861adde8 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=November 8, 2022 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Evers frequently uses the governor's [[veto]] power due to his opposition to much of the Republican-controlled [[Wisconsin Legislature]]'s agenda.<ref name="Vetoed">{{Cite web|url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/gov-tony-evers-vetoed-these-bills-they-could-be-reconsidered-if-tim-michels-elected-governor|title=Gov. Tony Evers vetoed these bills. They could be reconsidered if Tim Michels is elected governor|first1=Shawn|last1=Johnson|website=Wisconsin Public Radio|date=October 18, 2022|quote=Tony Evers' 126 vetoes since January 2021 help tell the story of the policy differences between Democrats and Republicans in Wisconsin.}}</ref> Under Wisconsin law, the governor's veto power is stronger than it is in other U.S. states. Evers has used his veto power more often than any other governor in Wisconsin history, and has used his [[Line-item veto in the United States|line-item veto]] power to change Republican-authored bills.<ref name="wpr.org">{{cite web | url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/tony-evers-tim-michels-agree-evers-veto-pen-only-obstacle-more-100-gop-bills | title=Tony Evers, Tim Michels agree: Evers' veto pen is the only obstacle for more than 100 GOP bills|work=Wisconsin Public Radio|first=Shawn|last=Johnson|date=October 18, 2022|access-date=February 14, 2025}}</ref><ref name="apnews.com">{{cite web | url=https://apnews.com/article/evers-veto-400-years-wisconsin-school-funding-67a7847e4a24ea86f7d16123356f770d | title=Wisconsin governor's 400-year veto angers opponents in state with long history of creative cuts | website=[[AP News]]|first=Scott|last=Bauer | date=July 6, 2023|access-date=February 14, 2025}}</ref> In July 2025, Evers announced he would not seek reelection in [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|2026]].<ref name="NBC20250724">{{cite news  | last = Edelman | first = Adam | title = Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers won't run for re-election in 2026 | url = https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/wisconsin-gov-tony-evers-wont-run-re-election-rcna220797 | work = NBC News | date = 24 July 2025 | access-date = 25 February 2026 | publisher = NBCUniversal News Group}}</ref>

==Early life and career==
Evers was born on November 5, 1951, in [[Plymouth, Wisconsin]], the son of Jean (Gorrow) and Raymond Evers, a physician.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/tony-evers-we-have-to-have-a-governor-that-values/article_b73a7a06-075c-5f95-b33e-2f1f820077f6.html|title=Tony Evers: We have to have a governor that values education |author=Matthew DeFour, ''Wisconsin State Journal'' |date=July 21, 2018 }}</ref><ref name="NewYorker2018">{{cite magazine |last=Strauss |first=Valerie |title=Why Education May Be the Issue That Breaks Republicans' Decade-Long Grip on Wisconsin |url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/dispatch/why-education-may-be-the-issue-that-breaks-republicans-decade-long-grip-on-wisconsin |magazine=[[The New Yorker]] |date=August 15, 2018 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> His first job was "as a kid, scraping mold off of cheese" in Plymouth. As a young adult, Evers worked as a [[caregiver]] in a [[nursing home]].<ref>{{cite web|title=GOP State Senate candidate is off base with claim that Wisconsin governor 'never had a real job'|url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2020/may/08/don-pridemore/gop-state-senate-candidate-base-claim-wisconsin-go/|access-date=April 26, 2021|website=PolitiFact}}</ref> He attended [[Plymouth High School (Wisconsin)|Plymouth High School]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://dpi.wi.gov/statesupt/about|title=About Tony Evers|date=November 6, 2012|website=Wisconsin Department of Public Instruction|access-date=December 21, 2018}}</ref> He earned bachelor's (1973), master's (1976), and doctoral degrees (1986) in [[educational leadership]] from the [[University of Wisconsin&ndash;Madison]].<ref>{{cite book |chapter=Tony Evers, Governor |chapter-url=http://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/misc/lrb/blue_book/2019_2020/030_elected_officials.pdf |title=[[Wisconsin Blue Book |Wisconsin Blue Book 2019-2020]] |publisher=Wisconsin Legislative Reference Bureau |page=4}}</ref><ref name="UWRegentsBio"/> He began his professional career as a teacher and media coordinator in the [[Tomah, Wisconsin|Tomah]] school district. From 1979 to 1980 he was principal of Tomah Elementary School, and from 1980 to 1984 he was principal of Tomah High School. From 1984 to 1988 Evers was superintendent of the [[Oakfield, Wisconsin|Oakfield]] school district, and from 1988 to 1992 he was superintendent of the [[Verona Area School District]]. From 1992 to 2001 he was administrator of the Cooperative Education Service Agency (CESA) in [[Oshkosh, Wisconsin|Oshkosh]].<ref>{{cite news |date=March 30, 2013 |title=Tony Evers |url=http://chippewa.com/tony-evers/article_b916d822-9999-11e2-8044-0019bb2963f4.html |newspaper=The Chippewa Herald |location=Chippewa Falls, Wisconsin |access-date=July 14, 2015 }}</ref>

==Department of Public Instruction (2001–2019)==
Evers first ran for state superintendent, a nonpartisan post, in 1993, losing to [[John Benson (Wisconsin politician)|John Benson]].<ref name="PolitiFactCareer"/><ref name="TMJ4Career"/> He ran again in 1997 and placed fifth in the primary.<ref name="BlueBook1997"/> He ran again in 2001 and finished third in the primary.<ref>{{cite web |title=Wisconsin Blue Book 2001–2002: Elections |url=http://images.library.wisc.edu/WI/EFacs/WIBlueBks/BlueBks/WIBlueBk2001/reference/wi.wibluebk2001.i0016.pdf |date=2002 |access-date=February 20, 2026 |website=[[Wisconsin State Legislature]] |archive-date=March 22, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160322110136/http://images.library.wisc.edu/WI/EFacs/WIBlueBks/BlueBks/WIBlueBk2001/reference/wi.wibluebk2001.i0016.pdf |url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Elizabeth Burmaster]] won the primary and the general election, and appointed Evers deputy superintendent, a position he held until Burmaster was appointed president of [[Nicolet College]].<ref>{{cite news |date=November 20, 2008 |title=Tony Evers running for state superintendent |url=http://lacrossetribune.com/tomahjournal/news/local/tony-evers-running-for-state-superintendent/article_6a667473-7a76-5be7-8a76-c4d5a3f61c88.html |newspaper=The Tomah Journal |location=Tomah, Wisconsin |access-date=July 14, 2015}}</ref><ref name="UWRegentsBio"/> While serving as Burmaster's deputy, Evers served as president of the [[Council of Chief State School Officers]].<ref name="CCSSOPastPresident">{{cite web |title=State Superintendent Named President-Elect of CCSSO Board of Directors |url=https://msachieves.mdek12.org/state-superintendent-named-president-elect-of-ccsso-board-of-directors/ |publisher=Mississippi Department of Education |date=November 21, 2016 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite report |title=Leading for Equity: Opportunities for State Education Chiefs |url=https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED577044.pdf |publisher=Council of Chief State School Officers |date=February 2017 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===State Superintendent===
Evers then ran again in [[2009 Wisconsin elections#State elections |2009]], this time winning. He defeated Rose Fernandez in the general election.<ref>{{cite news |last=Derby |first=Samara Kalk |date=April 1, 2009 |title=A quiet race, the Evers-Fernandez face-off for Superintendent generates little interest |url=http://host.madison.com/thecaptimes/a-quiet-race-the-evers-fernandez-face-off-for-superintendent/article_3b9419ca-23f7-5e5e-8dd2-70dddce5c53e.html |newspaper=The Capital Times |location=Madison, Wisconsin |publisher=Madison |access-date=July 14, 2015 }}</ref> In April 2013, Evers defeated [[Don Pridemore]] and won reelection.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://fox6now.com/2013/04/02/tony-evers-wins-state-superintendent-seat-defeats-pridemore|title=Tony Evers wins state Superintendent seat, defeats Pridemore|date=April 3, 2013|publisher=Fox 6|access-date=June 5, 2018}}</ref> In 2017, Evers defeated [[Republican Party of Wisconsin|Republican]] candidate Lowell Holtz, a former [[Beloit, Wisconsin|Beloit]] superintendent, with about 70% of the vote.<ref name="WPR2017">{{cite news |title=Tony Evers Wins Third Term As State Superintendent |url=https://www.wpr.org/education/tony-evers-wins-third-term-state-superintendent |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 4, 2017 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="AP2017">{{cite news |title=Evers easily re-elected Wisconsin schools chief |url=https://www.tmj4.com/news/local-news/tony-evers-easily-re-elected-wisconsin-schools-chief |agency=[[Associated Press]] |work=[[WTMJ-TV]] |date=April 4, 2017 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In 2009, Evers used government email accounts to solicit campaign donations during work hours. He and another government employee were fined $250 each for the solicitations.<ref>{{cite news |title=DPI chief Evers agrees to fine |url=http://archive.jsonline.com/news/wisconsin/63550287.html/ |access-date=June 24, 2018 |newspaper=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel |date=October 5, 2009}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=State Superintendent Fined for Campaign Solicitation |url=http://www.todaystmj4.com/news/local/63557462.html |access-date=November 27, 2012 |newspaper=WTMJ 4 NBC Milwaukee |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091008015153/http://www.todaystmj4.com/news/local/63557462.html |archive-date=October 8, 2009 }}</ref>

In 2014, Evers proposed a $1.7 billion increase in state funding for K–12 schools. Wisconsin Governor [[Scott Walker (politician)|Scott Walker]] did not include Evers's proposal in his state budgets, citing the cost.<ref>{{cite news |last=Meyerhofer |first=Kelly |title=Tony Evers calls for nearly $1.7 billion hike in state funding for K-12 schools |url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/education/local_schools/tony-evers-calls-for-nearly-billion-hike-in-state-funding/article_818334ab-6333-5e3c-bba7-87b4e2ab5ebb.html |access-date=October 21, 2018 |work=[[Wisconsin State Journal]]}}</ref> In 2017, Evers called for a tenfold increase in school mental health funding.<ref>{{cite news |last=Elbow |first=Steven |title=Tony Evers proposes 10-fold increase in school mental health funding |url=https://madison.com/ct/news/local/education/tony-evers-proposes--fold-increase-in-school-mental-health/article_6977035d-2fdf-5a9f-b2b9-f296b95756b2.html |access-date=October 21, 2018 |work=[[The Capital Times]]}}</ref>

In October 2018, a divided federal appeals court found that, because there was a nearby archdiocesan school, Evers had violated neither the U.S. Constitution's [[Free Exercise Clause]] nor its [[Establishment Clause]] when he denied [[student transport|busing]] to an independent Catholic school.<ref>{{Bluebook journal |last=Note |title=Recent Case: Seventh Circuit Holds Denial of Busing to Catholic School Under Wisconsin Statute Does Not Violate Free Exercise or Establishment Clauses |volume=132 |journal=[[Harvard Law Review|Harv. L. Rev.]] |page=2344 |url=https://harvardlawreview.org/print/vol-132/st-augustine-school-v-evers/ |year=2019}}</ref> The [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] vacated that judgment in 2020 and remanded the case for further consideration. After the [[Wisconsin Supreme Court]] answered a question of state law certified by the federal appeals court, the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit|Seventh Circuit]] ruled in 2021 that the denial of transportation benefits violated Wisconsin law and remanded the case for further proceedings.<ref name="StAugustine2021">{{cite court |litigants=St. Augustine School v. Underly |vol=21 |reporter=F.4th |opinion=446 |court=7th Cir. |date=December 20, 2021 |url=https://media.ca7.uscourts.gov/cgi-bin/rssExec.pl?Path=Y2021%2FD12-20%2FC%3A17-2333%3AJ%3AWood%3Aaut%3AT%3AfnOp%3AN%3A2808325%3AS%3A0&Submit=Display}}</ref><ref>{{cite report |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court Calendar and Case Synopses, May 2021 |url= https://www.wicourts.gov/courts/supreme/docs/oac/oralargcasesynopsmay2021.pdf |publisher=Wisconsin Court System |date=May 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>[[File:Superintendent_Evers_delivering_the_State_of_Education_Address.jpg|right|thumb|Evers delivering the 2012 "State of Education Address" in the [[Wisconsin Capitol]] Rotunda]]

==Governor of Wisconsin (2019–present)==
===Elections===
====2018====
{{Main|2018 Wisconsin gubernatorial election}}

On August 23, 2017, Evers announced that he would seek the Democratic nomination for governor of Wisconsin in 2018.<ref>{{cite web| last= Opoien |first= Jessie| title= Wisconsin schools superintendent Tony Evers launches campaign for governor| website= The Capital Times| date= August 23, 2017| url= http://host.madison.com/ct/news/local/govt-and-politics/election-matters/wisconsin-schools-superintendent-tony-evers-launches-campaign-for-governor/article_b4a367a1-a8c5-5c21-8a9c-02137fc8554a.html}}</ref> He cited his 2017 reelection as state superintendent with over 70% of the vote, as well as his criticism of Governor Walker, as key reasons for deciding to run. Evers launched his first campaign advertisement against Walker on August 28, 2017.<ref>{{cite web| last= Johnson| first= Shawn| title= Evers Campaign Ad Hits Walker on Foxconn| website= Wisconsin Public Radio News| date= August 28, 2017| url= https://wpr.org/evers-campaign-ad-hits-walker-foxconn}}</ref> Evers won the eight-candidate Democratic primary on August 14, 2018.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DeFour |first1= Matthew |title=It's Evers: State schools superintendent to challenge Scott Walker in November |url= https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/it-s-evers-state-schools-superintendent-to-challenge-scott-walker/article_eeaa00ad-5e99-52bd-9085-dedbd0e8da8e.html |access-date=August 15, 2018 |newspaper=Wisconsin State Journal |date=August 14, 2018}}</ref> On November 6, 2018, Evers narrowly defeated Walker in the [[2018 Wisconsin gubernatorial election|general election]].<ref name="AP2018Win"/>

====2022====
{{Main|2022 Wisconsin gubernatorial election}}

Evers sought reelection in 2022. His 2018 running mate, Lieutenant Governor [[Mandela Barnes]], instead chose to run for U.S. Senate.<ref name="APBarnesSenate">{{cite news |title=Wisconsin Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes joins US Senate race |url=https://apnews.com/article/government-and-politics-wisconsin-senate-elections-election-2020-874003bb009ecf23441d19955a496c84 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=July 20, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=White |first=Laurel |title=Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes Announces US Senate Campaign |url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/lt-gov-mandela-barnes-announces-us-senate-campaign |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 20, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In the August Democratic primary, Evers was unopposed and Brookfield-area state representative [[Sara Rodriguez]] was nominated as his running mate. In the general election, Evers and Rodriguez defeated the Republican ticket of Tim Michels and [[Roger Roth]].<ref name="MJS2022Win"/><ref name="AP2022Win"/>

===Tenure===
[[File:Tony Evers 191109-Z-HD478-967.jpg|thumb|Evers in 2019, greeting a [[U.S. Air Force]] troop who was returning from service in Afghanistan]]
Evers has frequently used his [[veto]] power to oppose legislation passed by the Republican-controlled [[Wisconsin Legislature]]. He has used it more frequently than any governor in Wisconsin history, and has used line-item veto power to rewrite Republican-authored bills.<ref name= "wpr.org"/><ref name= "apnews.com"/>

Evers's vetoes have included laws related to election procedures, government powers during a pandemic, education, federal aid, redistricting, guns, police and crime, abortion, social welfare programs, and regulations and licensing.<ref name= "Vetoed"/>

====Republican efforts to restrict gubernatorial power====
Before Evers took office, the Republican-led legislature passed new laws weakening the offices of the governor and attorney general. They targeted Evers's authority over economic development issues, required his administration to rewrite thousands of government documents, and required the attorney general to get legislative approval before settling lawsuits.<ref name= "lame"/> The legislature also restricted voting access. Governor Walker signed all the legislation over Evers's objections.<ref name= "White">Laurel White, [https://www.npr.org/2019/01/17/686327659/federal-judge-strikes-down-lame-duck-changes-to-wisconsin-voting-laws Federal Judge Strikes Down Lame-Duck Changes To Wisconsin Voting Laws], NPR (January 17, 2019).</ref><ref>Riley Vetterkind, [https://madison.com/news/local/govt-and-politics/scott-walker-signs-all-three-lame-duck-bills-into-law/article_09c0ed0a-0832-5320-ae12-97063316914a.html Scott Walker signs all three lame-duck bills into law], [[Madison.com]] (December 15, 2018).</ref> These actions were challenged as unconstitutional,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/aponline/2019/02/21/us/ap-us-wisconsin-lame-duck-lawsuit.html|title=Wisconsin Democrats File 4th Lawsuit Against Lame-Duck Law|work=Associated Press |date=February 21, 2019|via=The New York Times|url-status=dead|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190228065932/https://www.nytimes.com/aponline/2019/02/21/us/ap-us-wisconsin-lame-duck-lawsuit.html|archive-date=28 February 2019}}</ref> with some voting law changes struck down by a federal district court<ref name= "White"/> but later restored by the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit]].<ref name= "lame"/>

Also before Evers took office, Walker, the legislature, and the Wisconsin Supreme Court reduced Evers's ability to appoint new administrators. Before leaving office, Walker appointed 82 people and the legislature confirmed them. When a judge declared these appointments unconstitutional, Evers replaced 15 of Walker's appointees.<ref>{{cite web| url= https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/435390-wisconsin-dem-governor-removes-82-scott-walker-appointees-added-during| title= Wisconsin Dem governor removes 82 Scott Walker appointees added during lame-duck session| last= Daugherty| first= Owen| date= March 22, 2019| website= The Hill| access-date= March 25, 2019}}</ref> These new appointments were upheld by a [[Wisconsin Court of Appeals]] decision but overruled by the Wisconsin Supreme Court. Walker's appointees were reinstated.<ref name= "lame">{{cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2019/04/04/lame-duck-scorecard-where-the-wisconsin-cases-stand/3333903002/ |title= Lame-duck scorecard: Where the cases stand in the fight over GOP laws limiting Wisconsin governor |first= Patrick |last= Marley |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= April 19, 2019 |access-date= March 17, 2024 }}</ref>

The Wisconsin Supreme Court also allowed most of the lame-duck laws the legislature adopted, defeating lawsuits brought by the [[League of Women Voters]] and [[Service Employees International Union]]. The lawsuits largely hinged on the constitutionality of the legislature holding such votes in "extraordinary sessions"—special sessions not called by the governor. Such sessions are not explicitly authorized by the [[Constitution of Wisconsin]] or state law, so litigants contended that the acts of such sessions are not constitutional. The Wisconsin Supreme Court rejected those arguments.<ref name="LameDuckDecision">{{cite court |litigants=League of Women Voters of Wisconsin v. Evers |vol=929 |reporter=N.W.2d |opinion=209 |court=Wis. |date=2019 |url= https://www.wicourts.gov/sc/opinions/19/pdf/19-0559.pdf}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title= Wisconsin Supreme Court Upholds GOP Lawmakers' Lame-Duck Session |url= https://www.wpr.org/justice/wisconsin-supreme-court-upholds-gop-lawmakers-lame-duck-session |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=June 21, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Evers's problems with Republican appointees continued into his term. Late in Evers's first term, many Walker appointees refused to leave office when their terms expired.<ref>{{cite news| url= https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-scott-walker-c47de08ae9470c7c31dbfc3db9c047fd  |title= Many GOP appointees with expired terms still on state boards |date= September 29, 2022 |work= [[Associated Press]] |access-date= March 17, 2024 }}</ref> Evers appointed replacements, but Senate Republicans did not act on the appointments. Evers challenged the holdovers in state court, and the Wisconsin Supreme Court held that appointees whose terms had expired could remain in their positions indefinitely so long as the Senate refused to confirm a replacement.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://captimes.com/news/government/wisconsin-supreme-court-rules-walker-holdover-appointee-can-keep-post/article_18883dc1-2c10-536d-a02e-1eb21e545b6b.html |title= Wisconsin Supreme Court rules Walker holdover appointee can keep post |first= Jack |last= Kelly |date= June 29, 2022 |newspaper= [[The Capital Times]] |access-date= March 17, 2024 }}</ref> The Senate also rejected a number of Evers's appointees. By March 2024, it had rejected 21 of his appointees; in the previous 40 years, the Senate had rejected four.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2024/03/12/wisconsin-senate-rejects-8-appointees-including-2-uw-regents/72942298007/ |title= Senate fires 8 Democratic appointees, including 2 UW Regents |first1= Kelly |last1= Meyerhofer |first2= Jessie |last2= Opoien |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= March 12, 2024 |access-date= March 16, 2024 }}</ref>

In Evers's second term, Republicans sought to enact constitutional amendments to further limit the governor's powers. In 2024, Wisconsin voters were asked to vote on two amendments that would limit the governor's control over state spending. One would invalidate any spending decisions made by the governor or other agency that was not explicitly appropriated by legislation. The other would require legislative approval for usage of any federal funds sent to the state.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://wisconsinexaminer.com/2024/03/13/senate-finishes-session-by-firing-more-evers-appointees-approving-constitutional-amendments-education-bills/  |title= Senate ends session by firing Evers appointees, approving constitutional amendments |first= Baylor |last= Spears |date= March 13, 2024 |newspaper= Wisconsin Examiner |access-date= March 15, 2024 }}</ref> Voters rejected both amendments in [[2024 Wisconsin elections#Ballot measures|the August 13 primary elections]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Kremer |first=Rich |title=Voters reject amendments to restrict governor's power over federal funds |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/vote-reject-amendments-restrict-governor-power-federal-funds-august-referendum |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=August 13, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Wisconsin voters reject constitutional amendment referendums on federal funds |url= https://www.fox6now.com/news/wisconsin-constitutional-amendment-referendums-federal-funds |work=[[WITI (TV)|FOX6 News Milwaukee]] |date=August 13, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026|author-first1=Jason|author-last1= Calvi| author-first2=Scott|author-last2=Bauer}}</ref>

====First term====
In February 2019, Evers withdrew [[Wisconsin National Guard]] forces from the border with Mexico, where President [[Donald Trump]] had called for a "[[National Emergency Concerning the Southern Border of the United States|national emergency]]". Evers said, "There is simply not ample evidence to support the president's contention that there exists a national security crisis at our Southwestern border. Therefore, there is no justification for the ongoing presence of Wisconsin National Guard personnel at the border."<ref>{{cite news |last= Bernard |first=Katie |title=Wisconsin governor pulls National Guard from southern border |url= https://www.cnn.com/2019/02/25/politics/wisconsin-withdraws-troops-from-border/index.html |work=[[CNN]] |date= February 25, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Evers Withdrawing Wisconsin National Guard From US-Mexico Border |url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/evers-withdrawing-wisconsin-national-guard-us-mexico-border |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=February 25, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In February 2019, Evers's administration prepared a budget proposal that included proposals to legalize the [[medical marijuana|medical use of marijuana]] for patients with certain conditions, upon the recommendation from a physician or practitioner. Evers also proposed to [[decriminalization|decriminalize]] the possession or distribution of 25 grams or less of [[marijuana in Wisconsin]] and to repeal the requirement that users of [[cannabidiol]] obtain a physician's certification every year. Evers's marijuana proposals were opposed by Republican leaders in the Legislature.<ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title=Gov. Tony Evers Proposes Medical Marijuana Legalization |url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/gov-tony-evers-proposes-medical-marijuana-legalization |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=February 18, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Evers Announces Proposal to Reform Wisconsin's Marijuana Laws |url= https://evers.wi.gov/Pages/Newsroom/Press-Releases/021819-Gov-Evers-Announces-Proposal-to-Reform-Wisconsin%E2%80%99s-Marijuana-Laws.aspx |publisher=Office of the Governor of Wisconsin |date=February 18, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

On March 12, 2020, due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]], Evers declared a [[state of emergency|public health emergency in the state]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wpr.org/evers-declares-public-health-emergency-due-covid-19|title=Evers Declares Public Health Emergency Due To COVID-19|website=Wisconsin Public Radio|date=March 12, 2020|access-date=April 2, 2020}}</ref> The next day, he ordered all schools in the state to close by March 18, with no possibility of reopening until at least April 6.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://madison.com/ct/news/local/govt-and-politics/evers-orders-wisconsin-schools-closed/article_d0647b03-215d-5316-8502-89615c8e2832.html|title=Evers orders Wisconsin schools closed|website=madison.com|date=March 13, 2020|access-date=April 2, 2020}}</ref> On March 17, Evers instituted a statewide ban on public gatherings of more than 10 people, following an advisory from the federal government.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.tmj4.com/news/local-news/gov-tony-evers-orders-statewide-ban-of-all-public-gatherings-of-10-or-more|title=Gov. Tony Evers orders statewide ban of all public gatherings of 10 or more|website=tmj4.com|date=March 17, 2020|access-date=April 2, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url= https://www.cnn.com/2020/03/16/politics/white-house-guidelines-coronavirus/index.html|title=White House advises public to avoid groups of more than 10|website=CNN|date=March 16, 2020|access-date=April 2, 2020}}</ref> This was expanded to a statewide "safer at home" order on March 25, originally set to expire on April 25, with people allowed to leave their homes only for essential business and exercise.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.channel3000.com/evers-shares-details-on-safer-at-home-order/|title=Gov. Evers shares details on 'Safer at Home' order|website=channel3000.com|date=March 24, 2020|access-date=April 2, 2020}}</ref>

On April 6, Evers issued an executive order to delay the state's [[2020 Wisconsin Democratic primary|April 7 presidential primary]], as well as other coinciding elections. The move followed a dispute with legislative Republicans over whether to delay or otherwise modify in-person voting during the pandemic. Evers had said on April 2 that he had no legal authority to issue such an order, and Republican leaders in the legislature used his own words against him when challenging the order in court.<ref name="ElectionBlock">{{cite web |last1= Marley |first1= Patrick |title= High courts block Evers' Tuesday voting ban, restrict absentee ballots |url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/04/06/tony-evers-issues-order-shutting-down-tuesdays-election/2954626001/ |website= [[Milwaukee Journal-Sentinel]] |access-date= April 8, 2020 |date= April 7, 2020}}</ref> A conservative majority on the [[Wisconsin Supreme Court]] blocked the executive order just hours after it was issued on April 6, and the election took place as scheduled on April 7.<ref name= "ElectionBlock" />

On April 16, Evers ordered an extension of the statewide lockdown to May 26, and mandated all schools in the state to remain closed through the end of the academic year.<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://thehill.com/policy/healthcare/public-global-health/493177-wisconsin-extends-stay-at-home-through-may-26-closes| title= Wisconsin extends stay-at-home order through May 26, closes schools for rest of academic year| website= The Hill| date=April 16, 2020| access-date=May 2, 2020}}</ref> The legislature promptly sued to block the order, and the Wisconsin Supreme Court's conservative majority struck it down on May 13, after Evers's initial state of emergency expired.<ref>{{Cite web| url= https://www.wbay.com/content/news/Republicans-challenge-Evers-extension-of-Safer-at-Home-order-569825971.html/| title= Republicans challenge Evers' extension of Safer at Home order| website=WBAY| date=April 21, 2020| access-date=May 2, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| url= https://www.wisn.com/article/wisconsin-supreme-court-takes-case-challenging-gov-evers-stay-at-home-order/32348730/ |title= Wisconsin Supreme Court takes case challenging Gov. Evers' stay-at-home order| website= WISN| date=May 1, 2020| access-date=May 2, 2020}}</ref><ref name="MJS">{{cite news |last1=Beck |first1=Moly |title= Wisconsin Supreme Court strikes down Tony Evers' stay-at-home order that closed businesses, schools to limit spread of coronavirus |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2020/05/13/wisconsin-supreme-court-strikes-down-tony-evers-coronavirus-orders/5179205002/ |access-date=May 13, 2020 |publisher=Journal Sentinel Inc |date=May 13, 2020}}</ref> Evers responded to the suit by accusing legislative Republicans of a "power grab", and said they cared more about political power than people's lives. Republicans have called the extension an "abuse of power".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://wkow.com/2020/04/21/legislative-leaders-sue-over-safer-at-home-extension-evers-accuses-gop-of-power-grab/|title=Legislative leaders sue over 'Safer at Home' extension; Evers accuses GOP of 'power grab'|website=WKOW|date=April 21, 2020|access-date=May 2, 2020}}</ref>

On April 20, Evers announced a recovery plan called the "Badger Bounce Back", laying out details of his plan for reopening Wisconsin's economy gradually as the pandemic subsides. The plan called for daily death tolls from the virus to drop for 14 continuous days before "phase one" could be initiated.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gov. Evers announces Badger Bounce Back plan to reopen Wisconsin's economy |url=https://www.channel3000.com/gov-evers-announced-badgers-bounce-back-plan-to-reopen-wisconsins-economy/ |work=[[WISC-TV]] |date=April 20, 2020 |access-date=May 2, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Evers' 'Badger Bounce Back' Plan Offers Roadmap For Reopening State Economy |url=https://www.wpr.org/health/evers-badger-bounce-back-plan-offers-roadmap-reopening-state-economy |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 20, 2020 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

On July 30, Evers issued a statewide [[Face masks during the COVID-19 pandemic|mask mandate]] in a new attempt to curb the increasing spread of the virus, declaring a new state of emergency in order to do so.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wpr.org/gov-tony-evers-issues-statewide-mask-mandate-wisconsin|title=Gov. Tony Evers Issues Statewide Mask Mandate For Wisconsin|website=Wisconsin Public Radio|date=July 30, 2020|access-date=October 9, 2021}}</ref> Republicans promptly sued, arguing that he had overstepped his power. In February 2021, the legislature voted to repeal the statewide mask mandate. Evers countered by issuing a new statewide mask mandate.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-mask-mandate-d8436b7f9a7539e9d1cd2589483916d8|title=Wisconsin governor issues new mask mandate after GOP repeal|website=[[Associated Press]]|date=February 4, 2021|access-date=October 9, 2021}}</ref>

After the [[shooting of Jacob Blake]] in [[Kenosha, Wisconsin|Kenosha]] Evers issued a statement denouncing the [[Police brutality in the United States|excessive use of force by police]] and invoking the names of African Americans killed by law enforcement.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cbs58.com/news/gov-evers-releases-statement-on-shooting-of-jacob-blake-in-kenosha |title=Gov. Evers releases statement on shooting of Jacob Blake in Kenosha |date=August 23, 2020| publisher=[[WDJT-TV]]|access-date=August 24, 2020}}</ref> He said, "While we do not have all of the details yet, what we know for certain is that he is not the first Black man or person to have been shot or injured or mercilessly killed at the hands of individuals in law enforcement in our state or our country."<ref name=bbc>{{cite news|url= https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-53886070|work=[[BBC News]] |department=US & Canada|title=Kenosha shooting: Protests erupt after US police shoot black man|date=August 24, 2020|access-date=August 24, 2020}}</ref>

After the subsequent [[Kenosha unrest|unrest in Kenosha]], Evers deployed the [[Wisconsin National Guard]] to Kenosha.<ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.npr.org/sections/live-updates-protests-for-racial-justice/2020/08/24/905316709/wisconsin-police-shooting-leaves-black-man-in-serious-condition| title= Wisconsin Deploys National Guard After Shooting Of Black Man Sparks Protests| first1= Brakkton| last1= Booker| first2= Emma| last2= Bowman| publisher= NPR| date= August 24, 2020| access-date= August 24, 2020}}</ref> Looting and damage to vehicles, businesses, and public facilities such as schools, the [[Dinosaur Discovery Museum]], and a public library were reported.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.nytimes.com/2020/08/24/us/kenosha-police-shooting.html |title= Wisconsin Reels After Police Shooting and Night of Protest| first1= Julie|last1= Bosman| first2= Sarah| last2= Mervosh| work= The New York Times| date= August 24, 2020| access-date= August 24, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2020/08/24/kenosha-protests-escalate-after-police-shoot-black-man-jacob-blake/3430846001/ |title= Businesses damaged, vehicles burned in Wisconsin after Kenosha police officer shoots Black man |website= [[USA Today]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.kenoshanews.com/news/local/kenosha-residents-local-government-cleaning-up-in-aftermath-of-civil-unrest/article_2c0cafd8-e6d3-5526-9bd5-6a09a4e8ed6b.html |title= Kenosha residents, local government cleaning up in aftermath of civil unrest| first= Deneen| last= Smith|publisher= Kenosha News| date= August 24, 2020| access-date= August 24, 2020}}</ref> Evers also responded by calling state lawmakers into a special session to pass legislation addressing police brutality.<ref>{{Cite news |last= Beck |first= Molly |date= August 24, 2020 |title= Tony Evers calls special session on reducing police brutality after shooting of Jacob Blake |work= Milwaukee Journal Sentinel |url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2020/08/24/wisconsin-governor-evers-calls-special-session-police-brutality-after-jacob-blake-shooting-kenosha/3430490001/ |access-date= August 24, 2020}}</ref>

On March 31, 2021, the Wisconsin Supreme Court struck down Evers's mask mandate in a 4–3 ruling, holding that state law did not allow the governor to issue repeated emergency declarations for the same COVID-19 emergency without legislative approval.<ref name="APMask2021">{{cite news |last=Richmond |first=Todd |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court strikes down governor's mask mandate |url=https://apnews.com/article/legislature-madison-wisconsin-coronavirus-pandemic-strikes-9f05a7331f7957721799b23ea381fead |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=March 31, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court Strikes Down Statewide Mask Mandate |url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/wisconsin-supreme-court-strikes-down-statewide-mask-mandate |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=March 31, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Fabick v. Evers |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/misc/lc/issue_briefs/2021/covid_19/ib_fabick_evers_2021_02_04 |publisher=Wisconsin Legislative Council |date=April 2, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> The Wisconsin Supreme Court had previously struck down the state's May 2020 stay-at-home order; it was the first state supreme court to strike down such lockdown measures.{{citation needed|date=August 2026}}

On April 30, 2021, Evers sought $1.6 billion in federal funds to expand access to Wisconsin's Medicaid program. He also proposed legalizing medical and recreational marijuana, as well as increasing the minimum wage and granting public workers collective bargaining rights. Republicans in the state legislature blocked all the proposals.<ref>{{cite web|date=2021|title=A health-care change could bring the state $1.6 billion in federal dollars. Republican legislators are uninterested.|url=https://www.jsonline.com/restricted/?return=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.jsonline.com%2Fstory%2Fmoney%2Fbusiness%2Fhealth-care%2F2021%2F04%2F30%2Fwisconsin-republican-legislators-have-no-interest-1-6-billion-additional-federal-dollars-could-flow%2F7388332002%2F|access-date=April 30, 2021|website=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|date=April 30, 2021| title= Republicans to kill legalized pot, other Evers priorities|url=https://www.channel3000.com/republicans-to-kill-legalized-pot-other-evers-priorities/|access-date=April 30, 2021|website=Channel3000.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| title=Evers says he's listening to the people, not GOP lawmakers|url=https://apnews.com/article/business-government-and-politics-d53bf1b7fcc281713167539ce395fffc|access-date=April 30, 2021|website=AP NEWS|date=April 29, 2021 }}</ref>

====Second term====
After Evers's reelection in 2022, funding for local governments became a major issue for Evers and the Legislature. Municipalities of all sizes were struggling to make their budgets, with many threatening deep cuts to police and other services.<ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.wpr.org/economy/shared-revenue-program-discussions-state-local-leaders |title= Shared revenue discussions continue at the state level. Local leaders say the program needs to change |first= Evan |last= Casey |date= January 11, 2023 |work= [[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |access-date= March 17, 2024 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url= https://www.wuwm.com/2022-12-06/milwaukee-is-far-from-the-only-community-seeking-more-revenue-from-the-state-of-wisconsin |title= Milwaukee is far from the only community seeking more revenue from the state of Wisconsin |work= [[WUWM]] |first= Chuck |last= Quirmbach |date= December 6, 2022  |access-date= March 17, 2024 }}</ref> Evers and the legislature compromised on shared revenue in 2023 Wisconsin Act 12. The law increased state funding for all Wisconsin municipalities by at least 20%, while local governments outside Milwaukee received about 36% more overall. It also authorized the city of Milwaukee and Milwaukee County to impose additional local sales taxes.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.fox6now.com/news/wisconsin-shared-revenue-milwaukee-bankruptcy-evers |title=Shared revenue bill signed, Milwaukee sales tax hike possible |first=Jason |last=Calvi |date=June 20, 2023 |work=[[WITI (TV)]] |access-date=March 17, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Mentzer |first=Rob |title=Cities, towns 'might finally be able to make ends meet': Evers celebrates bipartisan shared revenue law |url= https://www.wpr.org/economy/evers-legislature-celebrates-bipartisan-shared-revenue-law |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=June 20, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2023 Wisconsin Act 12 |url=https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2023/related/acts/12 |publisher=[[Wisconsin Legislature]] |date=June 20, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In the 2023–2025 budget, Evers used his [[Line-item veto in the United States#Wisconsin|line-item veto]] to increase the limit under which school districts could request additional tax levies by referendum. In that budget, he also vetoed an income tax cut for the top two brackets of Wisconsin earners and an attempt to condense Wisconsin's four income tax brackets into three.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.vox.com/politics/2023/7/6/23786064/wisconsin-governor-tony-evers-education-funding |title=How Wisconsin's governor bested the GOP and secured education funding for 400 years |first=Li |last=Zhou |date=July 6, 2023 |work=[[Vox (website)|Vox]] |access-date=March 17, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin governor signs budget with partial vetoes, including 400-year school funding increase |url= https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-budget-university-cut-income-tax-9d37d68e8f312d6721b9f6d00d4d9fef |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=July 5, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Later that year, Evers and the legislature compromised on a funding package for [[American Family Field]] amid concern that Milwaukee could lose the [[Milwaukee Brewers]] if needed stadium repairs were not funded. The package committed public funding for repairs and required the Brewers to extend their lease through at least 2050.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wpr.org/politics/year-review-wisconsin-state-capitol |title=2023 at the Capitol: Shared revenue, Brewers stadium deal, elections |first=Mallory |last=Cheng |date=December 26, 2023 |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |access-date=March 17, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Richmond |first=Todd |title=Republicans tweak Brewers stadium repair plan to cut the total public contribution by $54 million |url=https://apnews.com/article/milwaukee-brewers-american-family-field-stadium-repairs-6fde38e1e04d2c29954e1f6a035f2b37 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=October 12, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Evers Signs Bills to Keep Milwaukee Brewers, Major League Baseball in Wisconsin Through 2050 |url=https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/WIGOV/bulletins/37e3e63 |publisher=Office of the Governor of Wisconsin |date=December 5, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

After the [[2024 Wisconsin elections#Legislative|2024 Wisconsin state legislative elections]], Evers called for the creation of a statewide [[Popular initiative|citizen-initiated referendum]] process.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Evers' plan to let Wisconsin voters repeal and create state laws meets GOP resistance |url=https://apnews.com/article/2be88328d8248737f602e20d625ee321 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=January 6, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Spears |first=Baylor |date=January 6, 2025 |title=Gov. Evers wants to let Wisconsin voters propose statewide ballot measures |url=https://wisconsinexaminer.com/2025/01/06/gov-evers-wants-to-let-wisconsin-voters-propose-statewide-ballot-measures/ |access-date=January 14, 2025 |work=Wisconsin Examiner}}</ref>

In his 2025–2027 budget proposal, Evers proposed changing gendered terminology in state laws governing artificial insemination and parentage, including replacing the term "mother" with "inseminated person" in some provisions.<ref>{{cite news |last=Liddell |first=James |title=Wisconsin governor defends change of 'mother' to 'inseminated person' in state law |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/wisconsin-governor-tony-evers-mother-inseminated-b2704186.html |access-date=February 28, 2025 |work=The Independent |date=February 25, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Beck |first=Molly |title=What to know about the 'inseminated person' language in Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers' budget |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2025/02/27/what-to-know-about-inseminated-person-language-in-tony-evers-budget/80493208007/ |access-date=February 28, 2025 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=February 27, 2025}}</ref><ref name="APParentage2025">{{cite news |author-last1=Bauer|author-first1=Scott|title=Wisconsin Republican compares himself to Trump as he launches bid for governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-bill-berrien-tony-evers-trump-a7a838f30d6453be1a1d29e33052a87f |work=Associated Press |date=July 9, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> Republican lawmakers removed the terminology changes before passing the budget Evers signed.<ref name="APParentage2025"/> Evers also proposed raising the cost of Wisconsin fishing licenses by $10.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Reid |first1=Claire |last2=Smith |first2=Paul |title=Here's how much Wisconsin fishing license fees would increase in Gov. Tony Evers' budget proposal |url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/sports/outdoors/2025/03/10/wisconsin-fishing-license-fees-would-increase-under-evers-budget-plan/81762170007/ |access-date=March 13, 2025 |work=[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date=March 10, 2025}}</ref> Evers and Republican legislative leaders later reached a compromise on the 2025–2027 budget, which Evers signed on July 3, 2025. The $111 billion budget included about $1.4 billion in income-tax cuts, increased funding for special education, child care, and the Universities of Wisconsin, and raised a hospital assessment intended to preserve federal Medicaid matching funds.<ref name="AP2025Budget">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Governor signs budget in early morning to secure Medicaid funds |url= https://apnews.com/article/bb279d4260373e98aedebd7ec9c9d143 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=July 3, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Kremer |first=Rich |title=In wee hours, Legislature passes and Evers signs 2-year state budget |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/wisconsin-state-budget-passed-gov-tony-evers-signs-2025-2027 |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=July 3, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=2025–27 Biennial Budget |url= https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/misc/lfb/budget/2025_27_biennial_budget/ |publisher=Wisconsin Legislative Fiscal Bureau |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In May 2026, Evers, Assembly Speaker [[Robin Vos]], and Senate Majority Leader [[Devin LeMahieu]] announced a further agreement on tax relief and school funding. The proposal passed the Assembly but failed in the Senate.<ref name= "WPR2026Deal">{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Shawn |last2=Kremer |first2=Rich |title=Evers, GOP leaders announce deal on tax relief and school funding |url= https://www.wpr.org/news/evers-vos-lemahieu-property-tax-deal-school-funding |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=May 11, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Johnson |first1=Shawn |last2= Kremer |first2=Rich |title=Deal to cut taxes, boost special ed funds, dies in Wisconsin Senate |url= https://www.wpr.org/news/wisconsin-senate-rejects-tax-cut-special-ed-deal |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date= May 13, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers, GOP leaders announce deal on tax relief and school funding |url=https://wisconsinwatch.org/2026/05/wisconsin-governor-evers-republican-leaders-budget-deal-tax-relief-school-funding/ |work=Wisconsin Watch |date=May 11, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In July 2023, Evers used his partial veto authority to extend certain school funding provisions for more than 400 years. The budget bill provided a $325 per-pupil increase in school district revenue limits for the 2023–24 and 2024–25 school years. By striking "20" and the hyphen from "2023–24 and 2024–25", Evers changed the end date to 2425. Wisconsin's partial-veto power permits the governor to remove words, numbers, and punctuation from appropriations bills. Republican lawmakers and business groups challenged the veto, arguing that it violated the state's 1990 [[Vanna White veto|"Vanna White" amendment]], which limited the governor's ability to create new sentences by combining parts of an appropriations bill. In April 2025, the Wisconsin Supreme Court upheld the veto in a 4–3 decision.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin governor's 400-year veto angers opponents in state with long history of creative cuts |url=https://apnews.com/article/evers-veto-400-years-wisconsin-school-funding-67a7847e4a24ea86f7d16123356f770d |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=July 6, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Spears |first=Baylor |date=2025-04-18 |title=WI Supreme Court upholds Gov. Evers partial veto extending school funding increases for 400 years |url= https://wisconsinexaminer.com/2025/04/18/wi-supreme-court-upholds-gov-evers-partial-veto-extending-school-funding-increases-for-400-years/ |access-date=2025-05-02 |website=Wisconsin Examiner |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court upholds the 400-year school funding veto by Evers in 2023 |url= https://pbswisconsin.org/news-item/wisconsin-supreme-court-upholds-the-400-year-school-funding-veto-by-evers-in-2023/ |access-date=2025-05-02 |website=PBS Wisconsin |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kremer |first= Rich |date=2025-04-18 |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court upholds Evers' veto boosting school funding for 400 years |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/wisconsin-supreme-court-upholds-evers-400-year-veto |access-date=2025-05-02 |website= WPR |language=en-US}}</ref>

====2026 campaign====
In July 2025, Evers announced he would not run for reelection in the [[2026 Wisconsin gubernatorial election]].<ref name="NBC20250724"/><ref name="APRetirement">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Democratic Gov. Tony Evers won't seek third term in battleground Wisconsin |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-gov-tony-evers-reelection-78b32ffc51dff53512fd7499f21e9878 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=July 24, 2025 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref>

After announcing his retirement, Evers said he was unlikely to endorse a candidate in the 2026 Democratic gubernatorial primary, adding, "I'll never say never, but I want to make sure who the rest of the candidates are before I even make that decision."<ref>{{cite news |title= Wisconsin's Democratic secretary of state won't run for governor but mulls lieutenant governor bid |url= https://apnews.com/article/9936e2a1db5c7be26fd0183ab55c50f0 |work= [[Associated Press]] |date= July 28, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.fox6now.com/news/race-wisconsin-governor-evers-talks-decision-rodriguez-launches-bid |title= Race for Wisconsin governor; Evers talks decision, Rodriguez launches bid |date= July 28, 2025 |first= Jason |last= Calvi |work= [[WITI (TV)]] |access-date= August 12, 2026}}</ref> Evers remained neutral for a year, as the Democratic primary field swelled to seven major candidates, including four current or former members of Evers's cabinet.

The campaign began to shift after the 2026 Democratic State Convention, in June, when Lieutenant Governor [[Sara Rodriguez]] won the convention's informal straw poll. In early July, Milwaukee County executive [[David Crowley (Wisconsin politician)|David Crowley]], who had reported strong early fundraising, withdrew from the race and endorsed Rodriguez. But days later, Rodriguez fired her campaign manager and announced significant financial reporting errors, and on July 17 she dropped out of the race. At the time, the ''[[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]]'' reported that Evers was "very seriously considering" a plan to bring Crowley back into the race.<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/07/17/tony-evers-considering-move-to-draw-crowley-back-into-governors-race/90955480007/ |title= Evers considering move to draw David Crowley back into governor's race |first= Jessie |last= Opoien |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= July 17, 2026 |access-date= August 12, 2026 }}</ref>

On July 18, Crowley reentered the race. On July 19, Evers—who was then on an official trade mission in [[Ghana]]—endorsed Crowley.<ref name= "APCrowleyEndorse">{{cite news |last= Kinnard |first=Meg |title= Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers backs David Crowley in Democratic primary |url= https://apnews.com/article/david-crowley-tony-evers-wisconsin-governor-a4ccb9e143e886e337d1f3b457b18207 |work= [[Associated Press]] |date= July 18, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1= Johnson |first1= Shawn |last2= Kremer |first2= Rich |title= Crowley returns to governor's race in last-minute effort to win Democratic primary |url= https://www.wpr.org/news/david-crowley-returns-governors-race-tony-evers-democratic-primary-2026 |work= [[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date= July 18, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref>  Polling at the time showed Crowley in third place, 30 points behind the frontrunner, state representative [[Francesca Hong]], who identifies as a [[democratic socialist]].<ref>{{cite web| url= https://law.marquette.edu/poll/2026/07/29/detailed-results-of-the-marquette-law-school-wisconsin-follow-up-poll-july-22-27-2026/| title= Detailed results of the Marquette Law School Wisconsin Follow-Up Poll-July 22 – 27, 2026| first= Charles| last= Franklin| date= July 29, 2026| access-date= July 29, 2026| website= [[Marquette University Law School]]}}</ref> Returning to Wisconsin only three weeks before the election, Evers began to campaign alongside Crowley across much of the state. The Crowley campaign announced surging fundraising and a rush of new union endorsements in the campaign's closing days. In the August 11 primary, Crowley prevailed by about 3,700 votes; political media called it a "stunning comeback" and a "big upset".<ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2026/08/12/david-crowley-pulls-off-stunning-comeback-victory-over-francesca-hong/91156642007/ |title= David Crowley pulls off stunning comeback victory over Francesca Hong |first1= Molly |last1= Beck |first2= Hope |last2= Karnopp |first3= Mary |last3= Spicuzza |newspaper= [[Milwaukee Journal Sentinel]] |date= August 12, 2026 |access-date= August 12, 2026 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/08/12/how-david-crowley-upset-francesca-hong-wisconsins-primary-governor/ |title= How mainstream Democrats pulled off a big upset against a democratic socialist |date= August 12, 2026 |work= [[Washington Post]] |first1= Patrick |last1= Marley |first2= Teo |last2= Armus |first3= Clara |last3= Ence Morse |access-date= August 12, 2026 }}</ref> Evers congratulated Crowley in the early hours of August 12, calling him "the future of our party".<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://captimes.com/news/elections/david-crowley-upsets-hong-in-democratic-primary-for-wisconsin-governor/article_5c10d77c-c592-4188-89c4-fcf6a45da3a9.html |title= Crowley wins upset, Hong concedes in Democratic primary for Wisconsin governor |first= Olivia |last= Herken |date= August 12, 2026 |work= [[The Capital Times]] |access-date= August 12, 2026 }}</ref>

==Political positions==
{{Modern liberalism US|politicians}}
===Abortion===
{{See also|Abortion in Wisconsin}}
In December 2021, as the [[United States Supreme Court]] heard [[oral argument]] in ''[[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization]]'', a case that overturned ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'', Evers vetoed five bills that would have restricted access to reproductive healthcare in Wisconsin, saying "as long as I'm governor, I will veto any legislation that turns back the clock on reproductive rights in this state—and that's a promise."<ref>{{Cite news |last= Dress| first=Brad| date=December 3, 2021| title=Wisconsin Democratic governor vetoes restrictive abortion bills| work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]| url= https://thehill.com/regulation/healthcare/584299-wisconsin-democratic-governor-vetoes-restrictive-abortion-bills| url-status=live| access-date=December 4, 2021| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20211204024623/https://thehill.com/regulation/healthcare/584299-wisconsin-democratic-governor-vetoes-restrictive-abortion-bills|archive-date=December 4, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last= Richmond |first=Todd |title=Wisconsin governor vetoes 5 Republican abortion bills |url= https://apnews.com/article/abortion-us-supreme-court-health-wisconsin-legislature-d93a00fc408f40da142a4a7856783da3 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=December 3, 2021 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Evers has also signed legislation supported by abortion opponents<ref name= "NRLCSafeHaven2026">{{cite news |title= Safe Haven Expansion Signed into Law in Wisconsin |work= National Right to Life News Today |publisher= National Right to Life Committee |date= 16 March 2026 |url= https://nrlc.org/nrlnewstoday/2026/03/safe-haven-expansion-signed-into-law-in-wisconsin/ |access-date= 24 September 2026}}</ref><ref name= "WRTLSafeHaven2026">{{cite news |title= Wisconsin Right to Life Statement on Safe Haven Expansion Signed into Law |work= L!fe Wisconsin Right to Life |publisher= National Right to Life Committee |date= 13 March 2026 |url= https://wisconsinrighttolife.org/wrtl-news/2026/03/13/wisconsin-right-to-life-statement-on-safe-haven-expansion-signed-into-law/ |access-date= 24 September 2026}}</ref> In March 2026, he signed legislation by Republicans [[Rob Hutton]] and [[Rick Gundrum]] that expanded Wisconsin's [[safe-haven law]].<ref name="Act94">{{cite web |title= 2025 Wisconsin Act 94 |url= https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/document/acts/2025/94 |publisher= [[Wisconsin Legislature]] |date= March 13, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title= Safe Haven law increases time for parents in crisis to surrender their newborn |url= https://www.cbs58.com/news/safe-haven-law-increases-time-for-parents-in-crisis-to-surrender-their-newborn |work= [[WDJT-TV|CBS 58]] |date= April 24, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref> The law raised the age at which an infant may be relinquished from 72 hours to 30 days and also authorized the use of newborn infant safety devices.<ref name= "Act94"/><ref>{{cite web |title= 2025 Wisconsin Act 94: Safe Haven Law |url= https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2025/related/lcactmemo/act094 |publisher= Wisconsin Legislative Council |date= March 16, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref> Wisconsin Right to Life applauded the law.<ref name= "NRLCSafeHaven2026" /><ref name= "WRTLSafeHaven2026" />

===Education===
During his 2018 campaign, Evers said he supported more funding for K-12 education and wanted to work with Republicans to do more to help underperforming schools.<ref>{{cite news |last=DeFour |first=Matthew |title=Funding for K-12 education a major fault line in governor's race |url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/funding-for-k--education-a-major-fault-line-in/article_f971531f-19ff-583e-b6c9-c189ba05225e.html |work=[[Wisconsin State Journal]] |access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref> He would like to expand Pre-K education to all students and continue the freeze of the in-state tuition price for higher education.<ref name="Sommerhauser 2018">{{Cite news|url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/what-would-the-next-four-years-bring-under-scott-walker/article_b6acf419-5647-5914-95c8-f8543b0c8bfe.html|url-access=limited<!-- not accessible from GDPR countries -->|title=What would the next four years bring under Scott Walker or Tony Evers?|author=Mark Sommerhauser|journal= Wisconsin State Journal|access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref>

In July 2023, Evers made a [[Line-item veto in the United States|line-item veto]] to the state budget for fiscal years 2024 and 2025 that enshrined per pupil increases in school funding of $325 a year until 2425. He did this by striking the hyphen and "20"s from where the budget bill mentioned the 2024–2025 school year.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.wpr.org/evers-state-budget-partial-veto-student-funding-402-years |title=Here's what 400 years of Wisconsin school district funding means |date=July 5, 2023 |website=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |access-date=July 7, 2023}}</ref><ref name="apnews.com"/> In April 2025, the Wisconsin Supreme Court upheld the validity of this veto; in the majority opinion, justice [[Jill Karofsky]] wrote that partial veto power is not constitutionally limited by "how much or how little the partial vetoes change policy, even when that change is considerable".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Mitch |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court Says Governor's 400-Year Edit Was Within Veto Authority |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/18/us/wisconsin-supreme-court-veto.html |access-date=20 April 2025 |publisher=The New York Times |date=18 April 2025 |language=en}}</ref>

===Gerrymandering===
Evers has criticized Wisconsin's legislative maps as "some of the most [[Gerrymandering|gerrymandered]], extreme maps in the United States", citing as evidence the fact that the state legislature has opposed policies such as legalizing marijuana and expanding Medicaid despite polls showing that a majority of Wisconsinites support both.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.tmj4.com/news/local-news/marquette-law-school-poll-shows-majority-are-in-favor-of-marijuana-legalization|title=Marquette Law School Poll shows majority are in favor of marijuana legalization|website=Today's TMJ4|date=January 24, 2019|access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref> In January 2020, he created a nonpartisan [[redistricting commission]] by executive order with the intent of drawing an alternative map proposal for post-[[2020 United States Census|2020 census]] redistricting to counter the proposal the Republican-controlled legislature has said it will put forward if the issue ends up in the state's court system, as it has under past periods of divided government in Wisconsin.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.wpr.org/gov-tony-evers-orders-creation-nonpartisan-redistricting-commission|title=Gov. Tony Evers Orders Creation Of Nonpartisan Redistricting Commission|website=Wisconsin Public Radio|date=January 27, 2020|access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref>

With the maps stuck in legislative gridlock, both sides sought relief from the courts. The Democrats sued in federal court; federal courts had handled redistricting in Wisconsin every time it had hit gridlock for the past 60 years. Republicans petitioned the Wisconsin Supreme Court, which had not handled a redistricting decision since 1964. The Court, with a 4&ndash;3 conservative majority, chose to assume jurisdiction and soon articulated that it would pursue a map with the least changes necessary from the existing one to bring it into compliance with the 2020 census figures. Complying with the Court's rules, Evers and legislative Republicans each submitted maps with only minor changes to the 2011 map. Either map would have left Republicans with significant structural advantages in the legislative elections. But Evers's map better complied with the Court's "least changes" rule, and so the Court's swing vote, Justice [[Brian Hagedorn]], sided with the Court's three liberals to adopt it. Republicans appealed the decision to the [[United States Supreme Court]], which struck down the legislative map in an unsigned opinion, criticizing the flawed process the Wisconsin Supreme Court had used, saying that it failed to properly consider minority representation issues that arose from Evers's map. But the high court did allow Evers's least-change congressional map to stand, since it had no VRA implications. On remand, Hagedorn sided with the court's three conservatives to reject Evers's map and select the Republican alternative. This map was used for the 2022 elections.<ref name="AP2022Maps">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court adopts GOP-drawn legislative maps |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-supreme-court-state-legislature-d9714f51739a01751aba0af674442f3a |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=April 15, 2022 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Johnson |first=Shawn |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court Chooses Maps Drawn By Republicans In New Redistricting Decision |url=https://www.wpr.org/justice/wisconsin-supreme-court-chooses-maps-drawn-republicans-new-redistricting-decision |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 15, 2022 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In April 2023, [[Janet Protasiewicz]] was elected to the Wisconsin Supreme Court, flipping the majority to the liberals. Democratic-aligned interest groups brought new lawsuits, challenging the constitutionality of the 2022 legislative districts. In ''[[Clarke v. Wisconsin Elections Commission]]'', the Court struck down the 2022 map on technical grounds and also found that the "least changes" rule had no basis in Wisconsin law or precedent and was therefore invalid. Evers was one of six parties to the lawsuit who proposed remedial redistricting plans. Court-hired consultants soon ruled out the two Republican proposals, saying they were both still gerrymanders. With the Court poised to select one of the four Democratic plans, Republicans in the legislature chose to embrace Evers's proposal. They first passed an amended version of the plan, seeking to protect a handful of incumbents, but Evers vetoed it. Republicans then passed Evers's original map without changes, and Evers signed it into law on February 19, 2024.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/evers-signs-new-maps-into-law-ending-wisconsin-redistricting-lawsuit |title=Evers signs new maps into law, effectively ending Wisconsin redistricting lawsuit |first=Rich |last=Kremer |date=February 19, 2024 |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |access-date=February 23, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court orders new legislative maps in redistricting case brought by Democrats |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-supreme-court-redistricting-eccbcfee414d1943073a9fb949743860 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=December 22, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Evers also joined a lawsuit in the Wisconsin Supreme Court challenging the 2022 congressional district map. That map had been created using the Court's now discredited "least changes" rule, so Democratic-aligned litigants argued that it should also be reconsidered.<ref>{{Cite news|url= https://www.wpr.org/politics/democratic-law-firm-files-challenge-to-wisconsins-congressional-maps  |title= Democratic law firm files challenge to Wisconsin's congressional maps |first= Anya |last= Van Wagtendonk |date= January 17, 2024 |work= [[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |access-date= February 23, 2024 }}</ref> The Court refused this case without explaining its reasoning.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin Supreme Court rejects Democrats' congressional redistricting challenge |url=https://apnews.com/article/52488bd0331dd9f8815946623fb3246a |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=March 1, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===Gun control===
Evers supports universal background checks for gun purchases. He has also supported an extreme risk protection order act, commonly known as a "[[red flag law]]", which would permit family members or police to petition a court to temporarily remove firearms from a person found to pose a danger to themselves or others.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wpr.org/gov-tony-evers-calls-special-session-gun-control |title=Gov. Tony Evers Calls Special Session On Gun Control |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=October 21, 2019 |access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref> Following the December 2024 shooting at [[Abundant Life Christian School]], Evers created a statewide Office of Violence Prevention with $10 million in federal funding.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin's governor creates new violence-prevention office in wake of school shooting |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-school-shooting-gun-governor-evers-962e0125cc05fdf3de8ea734e8124d62 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=January 14, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite press release |title=Gov. Evers Signs Executive Order Creating Wisconsin Office of Violence Prevention |url= https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/WIGOV/bulletins/3cc7c74 |publisher=Office of the Governor of Wisconsin |date=January 14, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In his 2025 State of the State address, he renewed calls for universal background checks and a red flag law and also proposed a ban on untraceable "ghost guns".<ref name="APGun2025">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin's Evers calls for gun control measures and a bipartisan approach to immigration |url=https://apnews.com/article/2d2f2b305d7f2bcebe996722259d050b |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=January 22, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===Health care===
Evers has said that Scott Walker's decisions about health care in Wisconsin led to higher insurance premiums for residents.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://wkow.com/news/2018/09/17/tony-evers-challenges-gov-walkers-record-on-health-care/|title=Tony Evers challenges Gov. Walker's record on healthcare|date=September 17, 2018| work=WKOW| access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref> He has pointed out that Minnesota accepted a Medicaid expansion and has been more proactive about healthcare overall, resulting in insurance premiums 47% lower than Wisconsin's.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/scott-walker-tony-evers-spar-over-cost-of-wisconsin-health/article_37597c12-03c7-5849-b5c4-3266299cb108.html|title=Scott Walker, Tony Evers spar over cost of Wisconsin health insurance|author=SCOTT BAUER|agency=Associated Press|work=madison.com|access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref> Evers supports legislation that would protect residents from higher costs for health insurance due to old age or preexisting conditions. He also supports allowing people to stay on their parents' health insurance plans until age 26.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/09/17/tony-evers-calls-scott-walker-drop-obamcare-lawsuit/1333835002/|title=Tony Evers challenges Scott Walker to drop Obamacare lawsuit|work=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel|access-date=October 30, 2018}}</ref> After taking office, Evers directed Attorney General [[Josh Kaul]] to withdraw Wisconsin from the multistate lawsuit challenging the [[Affordable Care Act]]; the state was dismissed from the case in April 2019.<ref name="WPRAca">{{cite news |last= White |first=Laurel |title=Gov. Tony Evers Directs Attorney General To Withdraw From Obamacare Lawsuit |url= https://www.wpr.org/politics/gov-tony-evers-directs-attorney-general-withdraw-obamacare-lawsuit |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=January 22, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title= Wisconsin Withdraws from GOP Lawsuit Attacking ACA |url=https://evers.wi.gov/Pages/Newsroom/Press-Releases/040919-Promises-Kept-Wisconsin-Withdraws-from-GOP-Lawsuit-Attacking-ACA.aspx |publisher=Office of the Governor of Wisconsin |date=April 9, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

Using funds from the [[Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act]] and the state Safe Drinking Water Loan Program, Evers's administration allocated more than $402 million in 2023 for drinking-water infrastructure, including lead-service-line replacement and projects addressing contaminants such as [[Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances|PFAS]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/pfas-pollution-water-infrastructure-bill-evers-39fede018c099d510fb1c02d8066090a |title=Evers administration allocates $402 million to combat PFAS, other water contaminants |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=October 23, 2023 |access-date=April 10, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Evers administration allocates $402M to combat PFAS, other water contaminants |url= https://www.wpr.org/environment/evers-administration-allocates-402m-combat-pfas-other-water-contaminants |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=October 23, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===Immigration===
Evers supports permitting undocumented immigrants living in Wisconsin to obtain driver's licenses, and has called this position "common sense".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.tmj4.com/news/local-news/gov-tony-evers-says-immigrant-drivers-license-proposal-common-sense |title=Gov. Tony Evers says immigrant driver's license proposal 'common sense' |work=[[WTMJ-TV]] |date=May 2019 |access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Chester |first=Jonah |title='Driver's licenses for all': Why some advocates call for expanding access in Wisconsin |url= https://www.wpr.org/politics/drivers-licenses-immigrants-documentation-farmers-driving-work |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=March 16, 2023 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In December 2019, in response to Trump's executive order requiring states' consent for refugee resettlement, Evers sent the administration a letter stating that Wisconsin would accept refugees, calling them "part of the fabric of [the] state", and criticizing Trump's refugee policies as "overly cumbersome and inappropriate".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/tony-evers-tells-trump-administration-wisconsin-welcomes-refugees/article_74078965-ae84-58ab-958a-53d5f222c08b.html |title=Tony Evers tells Trump administration Wisconsin welcomes refugees |work=[[Wisconsin State Journal]] |date=December 19, 2019 |access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref> In February 2020, Evers sent U.S. Secretary of State [[Mike Pompeo]] a letter asking him to halt negotiations with the government of [[Laos]] regarding deportations of Wisconsin's [[Hmong people|Hmong]] refugee population, who had previously been protected from deportation due to a long record of [[human rights in Laos|human rights violations in Laos]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wpr.org/evers-calls-trump-administration-halt-negotiations-hmong-deportations |title=Evers Calls On Trump Administration To Halt Negotiations On Hmong Deportations |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=February 13, 2020 |access-date=March 16, 2020}}</ref> In April 2026, Evers signed 2025 Wisconsin Act 240, which allows recipients of [[Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals]] to obtain state occupational credentials if they otherwise meet licensing requirements.<ref name="Act240">{{cite report |title=2025 Wisconsin Act 240 |url= https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/document/lcactmemos/2025/REG/Act%20240.pdf |publisher=Wisconsin Legislative Council |date=April 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |title=New Wisconsin law opens up professional licenses to DACA recipients |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/new-law-professional-licenses-daca-wisconsinites |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 14, 2026 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===Income tax===
During the 2018 campaign, Evers proposed to cut income tax by 10% for Wisconsin residents who earn less than $100,000 per year.<ref name="Sommerhauser 2018" /> He also pledged not to raise taxes, saying, "I'm planning to raise no taxes."<ref name="politifact.com">{{cite web |last=Litke |first=Eric |title=Evers reverses course on pledge to 'raise no taxes' |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2019/mar/05/tony-evers/tony-evers-reverses-course-pledge-raise-no-taxes/ |website=[[PolitiFact]] |date=March 5, 2019 |access-date=September 28, 2022}}</ref> Evers's first budget proposal in 2019 increased taxes by $1.3 billion, about 1.5% of the $83 billion state budget.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=2019-03-14 |title=Wisconsin Gov. Tony Evers calls $1.3 billion in tax increases 'small' |url=https://www.tmj4.com/news/local-news/wisconsin-gov-tony-evers-calls-1-3-billion-in-tax-increases-small |access-date=2022-09-28 |website=TMJ4 News |language=en}}</ref> His second proposal reduced his proposed tax increase to $1 billion.<ref>{{cite web |title=Yes, Evers' budget increases Wisconsin taxes by more than $1 billion |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2021/apr/06/rick-gundrum/yes-evers-budget-increases-wisconsin-taxes-more-1-/ |website=[[PolitiFact]] |date=April 6, 2021 |access-date=September 28, 2022}}</ref> Evers's tax increase included raising the gas tax, but the billion-dollar increase in state tax revenue came from reducing tax credits available to large manufacturers and wealthy tax filers with capital gains earnings of more than $300,000 per year. In later years, Evers fulfilled his proposal to cut income taxes by 10% making several line-item vetoes to specific elements, but largely leaving the budget as it was proposed by state Republicans.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.wpr.org/gov-tony-evers-signs-state-budget-including-gop-backed-tax-cut|title=Gov. Tony Evers Signs State Budget, Including GOP-Backed Tax Cut|website=Wisconsin Public Radio|date=July 8, 2021|access-date=October 9, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-06-29 |title=Highlights of Republican-authored Wisconsin state budget |url=https://apnews.com/article/wi-state-wire-wisconsin-state-budgets-business-health-0ca923c28dab64d1e721e7c2807afb49 |access-date=2022-09-28 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

===Infrastructure===
While campaigning for governor, Evers promised to focus on improving roads and bridges.<ref name="Sommerhauser 2018" /> In March 2024, he signed bipartisan bills to increase electric vehicle charging stations.<ref name="EV Infrastructure">{{Cite web |last1=Bauer |first1=Scott |title=Governor signs bills creating electric vehicle charging station network across Wisconsin |url=https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-electric-vehicles-charging-stations-8f240084670b7b210d162f83b22168d9 |access-date=2025-04-10 |website=AP News |language=en-US}}</ref>

===LGBT rights===
In June 2019, Evers issued an executive order to fly the rainbow flag over the [[Wisconsin State Capitol]] for [[Gay pride|Pride Month]], the first time the rainbow flag had flown over the building.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Asmelash |first1=Leah |last2=Morrow |first2=Allison |title=For the first time ever, Wisconsin is flying the rainbow pride flag over its Capitol building |url=https://www.cnn.com/2019/06/07/us/wisconsin-pride-flag-trnd/index.html |work=[[CNN]] |date=June 7, 2019 |access-date=June 8, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=White |first=Laurel |title=LGBTQ Flag Flying Over Wisconsin State Capitol For First Time In History |url= https://www.wpr.org/politics/lgbtq-flag-flying-over-wisconsin-state-capitol-first-time-history |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=June 7, 2019 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

On December 6, 2023, Evers vetoed a bill banning [[Transgender health care|gender-affirming care]] for minors. He wrote in his veto message, "This type of legislation, and the rhetoric beget by pursuing it, harms LGBTQ people and kids' mental health, emboldens anti-LGBTQ hate and violence, and threatens the safety and dignity of LGBTQ Wisconsinites". Evers said, "I will veto any bill that makes Wisconsin a less safe, less inclusive, and less welcoming place for LGBTQ people and kids."<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |date=December 6, 2023 |title=Democratic Wisconsin governor vetoes bill to ban gender-affirming care for kids |url= https://apnews.com/article/wisconsin-governor-veto-transgender-care-ban-68b0968cd63e20f5ce727b0c932ba4dd |access-date=April 12, 2025 |work=[[Associated Press]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Democratic Wisconsin governor vetoes bill to ban gender-affirming care for kids |url=https://www.wpr.org/health/democratic-wisconsin-governor-vetoes-bill-ban-gender-affirming-care-kids |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=December 6, 2023 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref>

In April 2024, Evers vetoed a bill that would have barred transgender high school athletes from competing on teams that align with their gender identity.<ref name="APTransSports2024">{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin governor vetoes transgender high school athletics ban |url= https://apnews.com/article/811748211df5ccf3b4ec8d18a70358b6 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=April 2, 2024 |access-date= August 11, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Evers vetoes transgender high school athletics ban |url= https://pbswisconsin.org/news-item/evers-vetoes-transgender-high-school-athletics-ban/ |work=PBS Wisconsin |agency= [[Associated Press]] |date=April 2, 2024 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref> In March 2026, he vetoed five additional bills that would have restricted transgender health care or participation in school sports.<ref>{{cite news |last=van Wagtendonk |first=Anya |title=As other Democrats moderate stances, Evers vetoes 5 bills restricting transgender health care, sports |url=https://www.wpr.org/news/evers-vetoes-trans-healthcare-sports |work=[[Wisconsin Public Radio]] |date=April 1, 2026 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

===Marijuana legalization===
Having campaigned on his support of [[cannabis in Wisconsin]], Evers announced in January 2019 the inclusion of [[Medical cannabis in the United States|medical marijuana]] in his state budget as a "first step" toward legalization.<ref>{{cite news |title=Tony Evers likely to include 'first step' to medical marijuana in his state budget |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2019/01/16/medical-marijuana-tony-evers-budget-likely-step-toward-legalization/2594419002/ |access-date=January 21, 2019 |work=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel }}</ref> On February 7, he announced he would propose [[Legalization of non-medical cannabis in the United States|legalizing recreational marijuana]] in his 2021–2023 biennial budget.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Marley |first1=Patrick |last2= Beck |first2=Molly |title=Gov. Tony Evers will propose legalizing recreational and medical marijuana as part of the next state budget |url=https://www.jsonline.com/story/news/politics/2021/02/07/tony-evers-propose-legalizing-recreational-and-medical-marijuana/4410636001/ |access-date=February 8, 2021 |publisher=Milwaukee Journal Sentinel |date=February 7, 2021}}</ref>

{{stack|[[File:Kathy_Evers_with_Tony_Evers.jpg|thumb|Tony and Kathy Evers in 2018]]}}
Republican legislative leaders have opposed Evers's proposals to legalize recreational marijuana, while some Republicans have supported more limited medical-marijuana legislation.<ref>{{cite news |last=Fannon |first=Emilee |title=In 2025, will lawmakers inch closer to marijuana legalization in Wisconsin? |url= https://cbs58.com/news/in-2025-will-lawmakers-inch-closer-to-marijuana-legalization-in-wisconsin |access-date= April 14, 2025 |work=[[WDJT-TV|CBS 58]] |date=January 1, 2025}}</ref> Evers again included recreational and medical marijuana legalization in his 2025–2027 budget proposal, but the Republican-controlled Joint Finance Committee removed the proposal in May 2025.<ref>{{cite news |last=Bauer |first=Scott |title=Wisconsin Republicans kill marijuana legalization and tax increases for millionaires |url= https://apnews.com/article/205546be49d94c34eb1d59b331dfe2b9 |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=May 8, 2025 |access-date=August 11, 2026}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Evers and his wife, Kathy, have three adult children and nine grandchildren.<ref>{{cite web |title=DPI About Tony Evers |url=https://dpi.wi.gov/statesupt/about |website=Wisconsin Department of Public Instruction |date=November 6, 2012 |access-date=May 9, 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20180430153556/https://dpi.wi.gov/statesupt/about |archive-date=April 30, 2018}}</ref><ref name="GovBio"/>

Evers had [[esophageal cancer]] before undergoing surgery in 2008.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://host.madison.com/wsj/news/local/govt-and-politics/tony-evers-seeks-a-third-term-after-battles-with-conservatives/article_9d40d7eb-8b83-56ca-9e63-8a69b56105df.html |title=Tony Evers seeks a third term after battles with conservatives, cancer and Common Core |first=Molly |last=Beck |work=[[Wisconsin State Journal]] |date= March 12, 2017 |access-date=July 12, 2018}}</ref><ref name="NewYorker2018"/>

